FILE: examples/spa/index.html, examples/spa/main.js, examples/spa/display/table.html,
      examples/spa/server.py, examples/spa/README.md
FEATURE: example-redesign
DATE: 2026-08-26
PRIOR STATE: as left by feature 'spa-example' / 'spa-route-exit-hook'

OLD_SECTION
index.html — 23 lines: an unstyled <nav>, a bare #contentBoundary and a <pre> log.
main.js    — routing, progress, callbacks and the ?autorun harness.
table.html — <table border="1" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="4">, unclassed buttons.
server.py  — /lib/vUX/ mapped to the repo root.

NEW_SECTION
index.html — 161 lines: shared design system, a sticky top bar with the nav, a styled
             #contentBoundary, two numbered sections (what to watch, bad input) and a
             diagnostics panel.
main.js    — the same routing and callbacks, plus markNav(), a log clear button,
             five validation demos and window.demoSpa.
table.html — button classes and a .table-scroll wrapper; presentational HTML attributes
             replaced by CSS.
server.py  — /shared/ additionally mapped to examples/shared/.

REASON
Brings the page up to the standard in CLAUDE.md. Routing, progress indication, the
per-link data attributes, the DataView table island, the exit hook and the ?autorun
harness are all unchanged in behaviour.

The design had to respect something particular to this example: route fragments in
display/ are fetched and injected at runtime, and they carry no classes of their own.
They are therefore styled through #contentBoundary — the element they are loaded into —
so a fragment stays portable content rather than becoming a styled component that only
works on this page. Stated in a comment and in the README.

server.py needed the /shared/ mapping because this example is served from its own root by
its own dev server, not from the repository root like the other three, so a relative path
to examples/shared/ does not resolve. Mapping it explicitly was chosen over reaching it
through the existing /lib/vUX/ mapping, which would have implied the stylesheet is part
of the library.

The ProgressIndicator's colours were part of the retheme and are easy to miss, since they
are config values rather than CSS: the bar kept trackColor "#eee" from the light design,
which on a dark header draws a bright white line across the top of the viewport. Now
"#5ccfe6" on "#1f2a3a", matching the page palette.

markNav() is new and does more than decorate: it highlights by location.pathname, which
makes the "User 8" opt-out visible — that link sets data-add-to-history="false", so its
content loads while the URL, and therefore the highlight, stays where it was. The
behaviour was already demonstrated; it was just invisible.

The five validation demos each build a THROWAWAY engine, so the live one is untouched.
Both initialize() guards were confirmed to run before boot() and startRouter(), i.e.
before the failing instance can attach any global handler.

VERIFICATION
The five error demos were checked against the real helpers and the engine's own guard
conditions: 5 assertions, all passing. The dev server was run and every URL the page
needs was confirmed to return 200 — /, /shared/example.css, /main.js, /pages/table.js,
the three display fragments, the library modules, and the two client-side routes /user/7
and /table through the history fallback. server.py re-checked for syntax.
